# ==============================================================================
# As a sender, strange characters and combinations are very suspicious.
# Here we check the sender for obfuscation patterns
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Language: -
# Weight:   12.0
# ==============================================================================

# Obfuscation patterns
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Detect standalone bidirectional control characters used to spoof visual order
# /[‮‬‭‪‫⁦⁧⁨⁩]/i

# Detect text wrapped in RLO/PDF markers (U+202E ... U+202C), a common masking trick
# /‮.{0,80}‬/i

# Right-to-left override (U+202E) in the display name
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sample: U+202E followed by "troppuS yfitopS" is displayed as "Spotify Support".
# The stored characters are the reverse of the words the reader sees.
#
# Only U+202E is matched. The broader bidi class above also contains marks that
# appear in ordinary mixed-direction names, and it stays commented out.
# A pattern that requires a closing U+202C misses this sample, because the
# override runs through to the end of the name.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
/‮/i

# Detect repeated invisible Unicode separator characters used as letter gaps or placeholders
# /[​‌‍﻿‏⁠]{4,}/i

# Mixed-script homoglyphs: one Latin letter directly beside a lookalike
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Spammers spell a Latin word with Cyrillic or Greek letters that look the same.
# Sample: "АDАС Vеrѕаnԁаbtеіlυng" reads as "ADAC Versandabteilung".
#
# These maps cannot ask for "a foreign letter somewhere in the word": Hyperscan
# has no Unicode script properties and no lookahead. The pattern therefore
# matches only an immediate pair, Latin then lookalike or lookalike then Latin.
# A space, hyphen, or other character between the two scripts does not match.
# A name that is entirely Latin or entirely Cyrillic does not match either.
#
# The symbol scores 12 and two characters are enough for a hit, so the class
# holds only letters that look like Latin. The rest of Cyrillic and Greek is
# left out on purpose.
#
# The flag u is required. Without it a character class matches single UTF-8
# bytes and hits unrelated text. The flag i does not case-fold Cyrillic or
# Greek, so both cases are written out.
#
# Cyrillic: А а В Е е К М Н О о Р С с Т Х х ѕ і ԁ
# Greek:    υ
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
/(?:[A-Za-z][АаВЕеКМНОоРСсТХхѕіԁυ]|[АаВЕеКМНОоРСсТХхѕіԁυ][A-Za-z])/iu

# Amazon with hidden Unicode separators like A‌m⁠a‌z​on\.co\.jp
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
/a(?:[​‌‍﻿‏⁠]+[​‌‍﻿‏⁠]*|)m(?:[​‌‍﻿‏⁠]+[​‌‍﻿‏⁠]*|)a(?:[​‌‍﻿‏⁠]+[​‌‍﻿‏⁠]*|)z(?:[​‌‍﻿‏⁠]+[​‌‍﻿‏⁠]*|)o[​‌‍﻿‏⁠]+[​‌‍﻿‏⁠]*n(?:[​‌‍﻿‏⁠]*\.[​‌‍﻿‏⁠]*c(?:[​‌‍﻿‏⁠]+[​‌‍﻿‏⁠]*|)o[​‌‍﻿‏⁠]*\.[​‌‍﻿‏⁠]*j[​‌‍﻿‏⁠]*p)?/i

# Apple with hidden Unicode separators
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
/a(?:[​‌‍﻿‏⁠]+[​‌‍﻿‏⁠]*|)p(?:[​‌‍﻿‏⁠]+[​‌‍﻿‏⁠]*|)p(?:[​‌‍﻿‏⁠]+[​‌‍﻿‏⁠]*|)l[​‌‍﻿‏⁠]+[​‌‍﻿‏⁠]*e/i

# ApoBank with hidden Unicode separators
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
/a(?:[​‌‍﻿‏⁠]+[​‌‍﻿‏⁠]*|)p(?:[​‌‍﻿‏⁠]+[​‌‍﻿‏⁠]*|)o(?:[​‌‍﻿‏⁠]+[​‌‍﻿‏⁠]*|)b(?:[​‌‍﻿‏⁠]+[​‌‍﻿‏⁠]*|)a(?:[​‌‍﻿‏⁠]+[​‌‍﻿‏⁠]*|)n(?:[​‌‍﻿‏⁠]+[​‌‍﻿‏⁠]*|)k/i

